.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1245 AM PDT Tue Aug 11 2026
(Today and tonight)

Cooling trend continues today as upper-level shortwave troughing
deepens off the West Coast. The associated lift will deepen the
marine layer and bring a chance for drizzle to coastal areas. The
Timber Fire in Big Sur will continue to bring smoke and haze to the
Central Coast via light southwesterly transport winds from the
aforementioned troughing. Ample moisture and sufficient instability
will bring up to a 10% chance for elevated rain showers tomorrow.
The relatively greatest chances are across the Interior Mountains of
Monterey and San Benito Counties. The primary hazard would be
erratic/gusty outflow winds with dry low levels and DCAPE near 1000
J/kg. Minor coastal flooding is expected during the highest high
tide (evening/overnight) for bayshore locations along the San
Francisco Bay, San Pablo Bay, and Monterey Bay through Thursday
morning.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1245 AM PDT Tue Aug 11 2026
(Wednesday through Monday)

A reinforcing trough Wednesday will bring widespread below normal
temperatures, deepen the marine layer further, and bring chances for
coastal drizzle through Thursday. It may also act as the trigger for
elevated rain showers/thunderstorms as it overlaps with an influx of
monsoonal/sub-tropical moisture and sufficient instability. The best
chances are across the Central Coast on Wednesday, more specifically
the southern and eastern portions. While confidence is low, this
would be a high-impact scenario with erratic/gusty outflow winds and
the potential for new fire starts. A gradual warming trend is
expected Friday through the weekend as upper-level ridging builds in
from the southeast; however, temperatures will still remain well
below normal.

