.FIRE WEATHER...
Issued at 529 AM PDT Tue Aug 11 2026

There is up to a 15% chance for elevated rain showers/thunderstorms
Wednesday across the Central Coast. Lightning was occurring with
a very slow moving 500 mb low pressure system located 400 miles
southwest of Monterey this morning. This low is forecast to move
very slowly northeastward today and tonight then start to move a
little quicker to the northeast Wednesday guided by strengthening
subtropical jet stream winds (polar jet stream winds will be located
to its northwest). This will be part of the evolving eastern Pacific
trough pattern this week mentioned in our discussion. We`ll monitor
this low closely for mainly mid level convective potential because
it`ll be located along and moving with a relatively sharp moisture
gradient (this can favor convection by locally steepening lapse rates
if there is upward forcing of a moist layer below a dry layer. The
good news is the very development of this trough this week will be
resulting in deep layered thermal troughing, increasing the marine
layer depth, the inland extent of marine influence including stratus,
fog and drizzle which will reduce surface convective potential inland,
that is, reducing the need for the atmosphere to do work and overturn
a more deeply unstable atmosphere (to restore stability). This bears
watching closely if only because summer season features moving north
from lower latitudes are complex, potentially dynamic changing features
even when 500 mb heights are greater than 580 decameters.

